# launch sweep -- 30 separate processes of one benchmark
# benchmark: ./build/benchmark/wordtype_narrow 
# launches: 30
# arch:     x86_64
# host:     AMD Ryzen 5 5600X 6-Core Processor
# kernel:   5.15.167.4-microsoft-standard-WSL2
# compiler: g++ (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04) 11.4.0
# commit:   80ff0a8
# governor: none (this host exposes no cpufreq)
# pinning:  taskset -c 2
# sleep between launches: 0s
# date:     2026-09-21T16:00:04-07:00
# loadavg at start: 1.37 1.79 1.95 1/1305 22651
# NO launch is discarded -- a cold start is part of what varies between
# launches, and trimming it would trim the tail this sweep measures.

### run 1
=== native 64-bit kernels, or narrowing? ===
640x480, 15 interleaved rounds, minimum reported
binCV SIMD: NEON=NO AVX2=yes popcount=hardware (fast paths active)

edgeThreshold, 8-bit in, 1-bit out
 (A) native uint32 buffer     17640 ns 1.00x
 (B) uint64 buffer, scalar fallback    679062 ns  0.03x
 (C) uint64 buffer, narrowed view     18340 ns  0.96x

 (A) vs (C), pixel by pixel: 0 of 307200 differ

 memory (row-stride rounding, the reason chose 32):
 level 0       640x480  uint32   38400 B uint64   38400 B +0.0%
 level 1       320x240  uint32    9600 B uint64    9600 B +0.0%
 level 2       160x120  uint32    2400 B uint64    2880 B +20.0%
 level 3        80x60   uint32     720 B uint64     960 B +33.3%
 the case       94x60   uint32     720 B uint64     960 B +33.3%

If (C) reaches (A), a native 64-bit kernel can at best MATCH it --
same kernel, same bytes -- so there is nothing left for it to win, and
the answer is guidance rather than a second implementation of every
gated kernel.
